WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
Welcome to Module 8, Professional Practices.

00:00:01.792 --> 00:00:07.932
In this module, we are going to focus on habits that make Python code more professional.

00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:10.552
Up to now, we have mostly focused on learning how Python works.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:15.692
We have written variables, loops, functions, data structures, and small analytical examples.

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:19.212
Now we are going to ask a different question.

00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:23.417
What makes code easier to trust, share, maintain, and improve?

00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:26.747
Professional code is not only code that runs one time.

00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:32.847
Professional code should be readable, reliable, tested, documented, reproducible, and respectful of data.

00:00:32.847 --> 00:00:37.597
This matters in analytics because our code often supports decisions.

00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:41.027
If the code is confusing, fragile, undocumented, or careless with sensitive information,

00:00:41.027 --> 00:00:44.187
then the analysis becomes harder to trust.

00:00:44.187 --> 00:00:48.322
As we move through this module, try to think like a future reader.

00:00:48.322 --> 00:00:53.967
That future reader might be your classmate, your coworker, your instructor, or even yourself two months later.

00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:57.777
The goal is not to make the code look complicated.

00:00:57.777 --> 00:01:00.594
The goal is to make the code clear and dependable.
